Z||000008||theogre||10-31-1999||11:24 AM||||Acording to rodney dickman, there are 3 sets od springs identified by colored paint. They get stronger as the deck lid gets heavier. The wing lids have the heaviest. Having handled a wing lid with the wing torn off, It was definatly stiffer than base springs.<P>Yes, you can "windup" the spring. I haven't done this on a Fiero so I can't tell you how reliable it is. Other cars I have done it to have had poor results. The lid may stay up, but the slightest breeze may drop it on you. This is problem enough on a Fiero stock configuration.<P>You can either replace the springs or add one of rodney's gas tube auxilary kits.||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phil||10-31-1999||01:37 PM||||Sorry about posting the same thing twice but the first one wouldn't show up when I checked  for it even the next day it was a no show. Has anybody had a similar problem?||||reg||1
Z||000010||HotFieroGT||10-31-1999||06:45 PM||||Just got back from the junkyard with a new decklid spring off of an 85 SE.  The spring diameter turned out to be smaller than on my 88 GT with spoiler.  As a matter of fact both of my 88 GT's had much thicker springs than my junkyard spring.  So I know there are at least two different sizes.||||reg||1
